  "semantic_description": {
    "level_1_elemental": "Heatmap with 4 rows (acquisition channels: Referral, Organic, Content, Paid Search) and 12 columns (weeks 0\u201311 since signup). Cell color encodes weekly retention as a percent of week-0 users, on a single-hue sequential blue ramp from 0 (palest) to 100 (darkest).",
    "level_2_statistical": "Week 0 is 100% for all rows by construction. By week 11, Referral retains 64%, Organic 53%, Content 31%, and Paid Search 15%. The steepest single-week drop is Paid Search week 0\u21921 (100\u219258, a 42-point fall); Referral's first-week drop is smallest (100\u219288).",
    "level_3_perceptual": "Color darkens top-to-bottom and lightens left-to-right, forming a clear gradient: the top two rows (Referral, Organic) stay mid-to-dark blue across all weeks while the bottom row (Paid Search) fades to near-white by mid-period. The decay is front-loaded \u2014 most of the lightening happens in weeks 1\u20133, then each row flattens into a slowly-declining plateau.",
    "level_4_contextual": "Channels acquiring intent-driven users (referral, organic) retain roughly 3\u20134\u00d7 better at week 11 than paid search, suggesting paid-search spend buys low-commitment signups that churn before activation.",
    "short_alt": "Retention heatmap, 4 channels \u00d7 12 weeks: Paid Search fades to 15% by week 11 while Referral holds 64%.",
